-0.0024662017822265625, 0.0240020751953125, 0.0230712890625, -0.0142669677734375, -0.0038814544677734375, -0.0164642333984375, 0.0169677734375, 0.0274200439453125, 0.00653839111328125, 0.0051116943359375, 0.02239990234375, 0.0094757080078125, 0.02838134765625, 0.023345947265625, -0.04168701171875, 0.014007568359375, 0.01297760009765625, 0.01534271240234375, 0.01442718505859375, 0.05377197265625, -0.0150909423828125, 0.009185791015625, -0.001102447509765625, 0.0219268798828125, -0.0129547119140625, -0.00998687744140625, -0.0012683868408203125, -0.04119873046875, -0.0192718505859375, 0.009368896484375, 0.032257080078125, -0.00799560546875, 0.0172119140625, -0.05596923828125, -0.020111083984375, -0.050506591796875, 0.0026531219482421875, 0.023284912109375, -0.0160369873046875, -0.0266265869140625, -0.019561767578125, 0.005092620849609375, 0.0290374755859375, 0.029754638671875, 0.00565338134765625, 0.000946044921875, 0.01059722900390625, -0.01442718505859375, 0.0152435302734375, 0.0014848709106445312, -0.01415252685546875, -0.0003712177276611328, 0.0296478271484375, -0.022186279296875, 0.05255126953125, 0.00618743896484375, -0.0202484130859375, 0.031646728515625, 0.0248870849609375, -0.00958251953125, 0.004886627197265625, 0.027740478515625, 0.0217437744140625, 0.0224456787109375, -0.01160430908203125, -0.02349853515625, -0.03961181640625, -0.0008039474487304688, -0.01457977294921875, -0.0222930908203125, -0.01477813720703125, -0.00396728515625, 0.03271484375, -0.027313232421875, 0.027191162109375, -0.0235443115234375, -0.0167694091796875, 0.01007843017578125, -0.01531982421875, 0.0183868408203125, 0.01001739501953125, 0.004169464111328125, -0.00905609130859375, -0.0016021728515625, -0.01248931884765625, -0.00640106201171875, 0.01520538330078125, -0.004657745361328125, 0.0244140625, -0.00733184814453125, -0.0164794921875, 0.024322509765625, -0.0119476318359375, -0.004215240478515625, -0.0263824462890625, -0.0293731689453125, -0.0025768280029296875, 0.003040313720703125, 0.003116607666015625, 0.02642822265625, -0.007568359375, 0.007602691650390625, -0.008697509765625, 0.0014619827270507812, 0.01751708984375, -0.03277587890625, -0.00732421875, 0.034454345703125, -0.0203399658203125, 0.037078857421875, -0.0178985595703125, 0.003772735595703125, 0.0248260498046875, 0.01403045654296875, -0.0003349781036376953, 0.0013914108276367188, 0.025970458984375, -0.0005130767822265625, -0.0010433197021484375, -0.0124969482421875, -0.018341064453125, -0.036376953125, -0.03240966796875, 0.026153564453125, -0.02362060546875, 0.00099945068359375, 0.003978729248046875, -0.00012493133544921875, -0.004123687744140625, -0.02044677734375, 0.03448486328125, 0.01190948486328125, 0.031982421875, -0.0285797119140625, -0.0178985595703125, -0.00872039794921875, -0.03778076171875, -0.01000213623046875, -0.000579833984375, 0.0228424072265625, -0.01068878173828125, -0.0028858184814453125, -0.046356201171875, -0.018585205078125, 0.0204620361328125, 0.0151519775390625, 0.003635406494140625, -0.01332855224609375, -0.04449462890625, 0.02587890625, 0.01043701171875, 0.010986328125, -0.0033626556396484375, -0.038665771484375, 0.0102691650390625, -0.0016307830810546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. This overview aims to define jazz, outline its historical roots, highlight its key characteristics, and discuss its enduring influence. By tracing its development and ongoing relevance, we can better appreciate jazz\u2019s unique place in musical history. To understand jazz\u2019s journey, we will move from its origins and stylistic evolution to its present-day significance, connecting each stage to the next.\n\n**Definition and Historical Roots**\n\nJazz developed from a blend of musical traditions, including blues and ragtime, as well as European harmonic structures and African rhythmic practices. The blues contributed expressive melodies and the use of \u201cblue notes\u201d\u2014slightly lowered pitches that convey emotion and tension, often heard in vocal lines or instrumental solos. Ragtime introduced syncopated rhythms and lively piano styles, characterized by off-beat accents that create a sense of forward motion. European music provided harmonic frameworks, such as chord progressions and formal structures, while African traditions brought polyrhythms\u2014the simultaneous use of contrasting rhythms, as in drumming patterns\u2014and call and response patterns, where a musical phrase (the \u201ccall\u201d) is answered by another instrument or group (the \u201cresponse\u201d), fostering dialogue within the ensemble. Spirituals and hymns added soulful melodies and communal singing, marches contributed structured forms and brass instrumentation, and vaudeville songs and dance music influenced jazz\u2019s early repertoire and performance style.\n\n**The Jazz Age and Subgenres**\n\nAs jazz evolved, it entered the 1920s\u2014a period often called the Jazz Age\u2014when it gained widespread recognition and popularity in the United States. This rise was fueled by the migration of musicians to cities like Chicago and New York, the growth of radio and recording technology, and the embrace of jazz in dance halls and nightclubs. During this era, jazz became a major form of musical expression, influencing both concert music and popular genres.\n\nWithin this context, several subgenres emerged, each contributing distinct elements to jazz\u2019s evolution. Swing and big band music, for example, featured larger ensembles, arranged sections, and a strong rhythmic drive suitable for dancing, with bandleaders like Duke Ellington and Count Basie shaping the sound of the era. Hard bop, which built on bebop, incorporated blues and gospel influences, resulting in a more soulful and earthy sound exemplified by artists like Art Blakey and Horace Silver. Free jazz, pioneered by Ornette Coleman, broke away from fixed chord progressions and traditional forms, emphasizing collective improvisation and emotional intensity. Latin jazz blended jazz improvisation with Afro-Cuban and Brazilian rhythms, as heard in the work of Tito Puente and Stan Getz, introducing new percussion instruments and rhythmic patterns. These subgenres are highlighted for their historical impact and their roles in expanding jazz\u2019s stylistic boundaries.\n\n**Key Characteristics**\n\nTo understand jazz\u2019s distinctiveness, it is important to distinguish between features common to the genre and those specific to certain eras or styles. The following table summarizes these distinctions:\n\n| Characteristic         | Universal to Jazz         | Specific to Styles/Periods                | Example/Explanation                                      |\n|-----------------------|--------------------------|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Improvisation         | Yes                      | Varies in approach                        | Soloists create new melodies on the spot                 |\n| Swing Rhythm          | Often, but not always    | Prominent in swing, big band              | Danceable groove in Count Basie\u2019s music                  |\n| Polyrhythms           | Common                   | More pronounced in some styles            | Layered rhythms in African-influenced jazz               |\n| Call and Response     | Common                   | Varies in prominence                      | Trumpet and saxophone trading phrases                    |\n| Instrumentation       | Typical core ensemble    | Expanded in big band, fusion, Latin jazz  | Addition of electric instruments in fusion               |\n\nImprovisation\u2014the spontaneous creation of new melodies and variations during performance\u2014is central to nearly all jazz, allowing musicians to express individuality within a group context. \u201cSwing\u201d refers to a rhythmic groove that encourages movement and dance, but not all jazz employs swing rhythms; for example, bebop and free jazz often feature more complex or irregular rhythmic feels. Polyrhythms add complexity by layering multiple rhythms, as in the interplay between drums and piano. Call and response patterns foster interaction between musicians, such as a trumpet \u201ccalling\u201d a phrase and a saxophone \u201cresponding.\u201d Jazz ensembles typically include instruments like trumpet, saxophone, piano, double bass, and drums, though instrumentation varies by subgenre.\n\nLandmark recordings such as Miles Davis\u2019s \u201cKind of Blue\u201d (1959) introduced modal improvisation\u2014a technique where improvisers base their solos on scales (modes) rather than chord changes, allowing for greater melodic freedom and a more open sound. John Coltrane\u2019s \u201cA Love Supreme\u201d (1965) is noted for its spiritual depth and innovative structure, blending intense improvisation with a suite-like form that elevated jazz to new artistic heights. These recordings are significant for redefining jazz\u2019s possibilities and influencing generations of musicians.\n\n**Evolution and Influence**\n\nBuilding on its early foundations, jazz continued to evolve through the decades. Bebop, emerging in the 1940s, featured fast tempos, complex harmonies, and virtuosic improvisation, as heard in the work of Dizzy Gillespie and Thelonious Monk, who expanded the harmonic and rhythmic vocabulary of jazz and shifted its focus from dance music to art music. Cool jazz, popularized by artists like Dave Brubeck and Chet Baker, offered a more relaxed, smooth sound with subtle dynamics and intricate arrangements, appealing to new audiences. Fusion, developed in the late 1960s and 1970s by musicians such as Herbie Hancock and Weather Report, blended jazz with rock, funk, and electronic elements, opening new possibilities for instrumentation and audience reach.\n\nJazz\u2019s influence extends far beyond its own boundaries. For example, the development of Japanese jazz fusion in the 1970s, led by artists like Sadao Watanabe and the band Casiopea, demonstrates how jazz techniques and improvisation were adapted to local musical traditions. In Brazil, bossa nova emerged as a fusion of samba and jazz, with musicians like Jo\u00e3o Gilberto and Antonio Carlos Jobim collaborating with American jazz artists. In contemporary popular music, hip-hop producers sample jazz recordings, and artists such as Kendrick Lamar have incorporated jazz musicians and improvisational elements into their albums, as on \u201cTo Pimp a Butterfly.\u201d These examples illustrate jazz\u2019s global reach and its capacity to inspire innovation across genres.\n\n**Contemporary Relevance**\n\nToday, jazz remains a vital and innovative force in music, continually interacting with modern genres and cultures. Contemporary artists like Esperanza Spalding and Kamasi Washington expand the genre\u2019s boundaries by incorporating elements of hip-hop, R&B, and world music into their work. For example, Robert Glasper\u2019s collaborations with hip-hop artists and the blending of jazz with electronic music in projects like BADBADNOTGOOD illustrate jazz\u2019s adaptability. Jazz festivals around the world, such as the Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland and the Tokyo Jazz Festival in Japan, attract diverse audiences and showcase international talent. Educational programs, from university jazz studies to community workshops, foster new generations of musicians. Collaborations across genres and cultures, such as jazz\u2019s influence on Japanese jazz fusion or its integration into film scores and popular music, further demonstrate its ongoing significance and adaptability.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nFrom its origins in New Orleans to its global presence today, jazz has continually reinvented itself while maintaining core values of improvisation, collaboration, and innovation. The genre\u2019s rich history of stylistic evolution and cultural exchange has allowed it to influence and absorb elements from countless musical traditions. As jazz faces ongoing debates about tradition versus innovation and strives to engage new audiences in a changing musical landscape, its enduring spirit of creativity ensures that it will remain both a reflection of its roots and a dynamic, forward-looking art form."}}